{
 "lead": "Normal SI combustion is a single flame front that starts at the spark and sweeps the whole charge. Abnormal combustion is any burning that breaks this pattern: part of the charge ignites by itself, ahead of the front or before the spark. The two main forms are knock, where the end gas autoignites after the spark, and surface ignition, where a hot spot lights the charge, often before the spark (pre-ignition). Both raise pressure suddenly, cost power and, if they persist, damage pistons, rings and gaskets.",
 "prereq": "You need: the end gas and flame propagation (<a href=\"#stages-of-combustion-and-flame-propagation-in-si-engines\">stages of combustion</a>), and the rate of pressure rise (<a href=\"#flame-velocity-flame-front-and-rate-of-pressure-rise\">flame velocity</a>).",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "The race between the flame and the end gas",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "In knock the spark-plug flame still burns most of the charge normally. The trouble is the <b>end gas</b>, the last 5 to 25 % of the charge, which lies farthest from the plug. As the front advances, the expanding burnt gas and the rising piston compress the end gas, and its temperature climbs. Like any fuel–air mixture held hot, it starts pre-flame reactions and will ignite by itself after a delay, the <b>end-gas ignition delay</b> τ."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "So there is a race. If the flame reaches the end gas before τ runs out, the end gas burns normally. If τ runs out first, the end gas autoignites at many points almost at once. That near-instant release of energy in a small volume sends pressure waves across the chamber; they reflect from the walls and ring at a few kHz, heard as a metallic <b>pinking</b> or <b>knock</b>. This is the <b>autoignition theory</b> of knock, the explanation accepted today."
    },
    {
     "type": "bullets",
     "items": [
      "Knock is helped by anything that <b>shortens τ</b> (a hotter, denser end gas, a low-octane fuel) or <b>lengthens the flame's travel time</b> (a long flame path, little turbulence). The full list is in <a href=\"#factors-affecting-knock\">factors affecting knock</a>.",
      "The ringing frequency is set by the bore and the speed of sound in the hot gas: f ≈ 1.84 c ÷ (π B) for the first mode across the bore, about 6 to 7 kHz in a car engine. Knock sensors listen for it."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Knock, detonation and surface ignition",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Knock</b> (spark knock) begins after the spark, in the end gas. On a p–θ trace it shows as high-frequency oscillations near and after peak pressure."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Detonation.</b> Many textbooks use \"detonation\" as another word for knock. Strictly, a detonation is a combustion wave driven by a shock, moving faster than sound in the unburnt gas. In heavy knock the end-gas reaction can approach this, with very high local pressures that erode the piston crown. If a question separates the two, call detonation the severe, shock-driven form of knock."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Surface ignition</b> is ignition by a hot surface: an overheated plug electrode or insulator, a glowing carbon deposit, a hot exhaust valve or a sharp edge. If it happens <b>before the spark</b> it is <b>pre-ignition</b>; if after the spark, post-ignition, which simply adds a second flame front. Pre-ignition is the dangerous one. It starts the burn too early, so the charge is compressed while burning, peak pressure and temperature rise steeply near TDC, more heat flows into the metal, the hot spot gets hotter and the next cycle pre-ignites earlier still. This runaway can hole a piston in minutes."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Two related effects: <b>run-on</b> (dieseling), where a hot spot keeps the engine firing after the ignition is switched off; and <b>rumble</b>, a low-frequency roughness from several deposit-ignited fronts in high-compression engines. Pre-ignition and knock feed each other: pre-ignition heats the end gas and causes knock, and repeated knock overheats surfaces and causes pre-ignition."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Effects and how to tell them apart",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "bullets",
     "items": [
      "<b>Noise and vibration:</b> the ping of knock; a dull thud from pre-ignition.",
      "<b>Mechanical damage:</b> pitted or holed piston crowns, broken ring lands, blown head gaskets, bearing wear.",
      "<b>Higher heat transfer:</b> the pressure waves scrub away the cooler boundary layer, so the walls run hotter and power and efficiency fall.",
      "<b>Carbon deposits and pre-ignition</b> follow from the overheating."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "To classify an event, mark the spark angle on the trace and compare times, not signs. An event at 6° bTDC is <i>after</i> a spark at 18° bTDC, because 6° bTDC comes later in the cycle. Ignition at 22° bTDC with the same spark is <i>before</i> it, so it is pre-ignition. Retarding the spark cures knock; it does not cure pre-ignition, because the hot spot does not wait for the spark."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "An SI engine (bore 86 mm) runs at 3000 rev/min. At TDC the flame front still has 20 mm to travel to the end gas, and it travels at 25 m/s. The end gas, at the state it then reaches, has an ignition delay of 0.6 ms on the present fuel and 1.0 ms on a higher-octane fuel. (a) Does the engine knock on each fuel? (b) If it knocks, find the knock frequency for a speed of sound of 950 m/s in the hot gas, and the crank angle per oscillation.",
   "steps": [
    "Degrees per ms at 3000 rev/min: 0.006 × 3000 = 18 °/ms.",
    "Flame travel time: 0.020 m ÷ 25 m/s = 0.0008 s = 0.8 ms, that is 0.8 × 18 = 14.4°. The flame reaches the end gas at 14.4° aTDC.",
    "Present fuel: τ = 0.6 ms = 10.8°, so the end gas autoignites at 10.8° aTDC, 3.6° before the flame arrives: the engine knocks.",
    "Higher-octane fuel: τ = 1.0 ms = 18°, so autoignition would come at 18° aTDC; the flame burns the end gas first, at 14.4° aTDC: no knock.",
    "Knock frequency: f = 1.84 × 950 ÷ (π × 0.086) = 1748 ÷ 0.2702 = 6470 Hz, about 6.5 kHz.",
    "One period: 1 ÷ 6470 = 0.155 ms, which is 0.155 × 18 = 2.8° of crank rotation."
   ],
   "answer": "(a) The engine knocks on the present fuel (end gas ignites at 10.8° aTDC, flame due at 14.4° aTDC) but not on the higher-octane fuel. (b) The cylinder rings at about 6.5 kHz, one oscillation every 2.8° of crank angle, as drawn in the figure."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "In knock the spark flame still burns most of the charge; only the end gas autoignites. Do not write that knock is \"burning that does not start at the plug\".",
  "Knock starts after the spark; pre-ignition starts before it. Compare the event with the spark angle, not with TDC.",
  "Retarding the spark reduces knock but does not stop pre-ignition, which is lit by a hot spot.",
  "Knock is not only a petrol-engine problem: CI engines also knock, from a long ignition delay at the start of combustion (see <a href=\"#diesel-knock-and-its-control\">diesel knock</a>).",
  "If a question asks for the shock-driven, most severe form of knock, answer detonation; elsewhere the two words are used for the same thing."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Define knock in an SI engine.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "Autoignition of the end gas ahead of the flame front, after the spark, giving pressure oscillations and a metallic noise."
  },
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Name three hot spots that can cause surface ignition.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "An overheated spark-plug electrode or insulator, glowing carbon deposits, a hot exhaust valve or a sharp metal edge."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "State the autoignition theory of knock in two sentences.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "The end gas, compressed by the burnt gas and the piston, autoignites if its ignition delay runs out before the flame reaches it. The near-instant burning of that gas sends pressure waves round the chamber, heard as knock."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why is pre-ignition more dangerous than mild knock?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "It starts combustion early, so the charge burns while being compressed; peak pressure and heat flux rise, the hot spot heats further, and each cycle pre-ignites earlier until a piston fails."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "Spark at 20° bTDC. Pressure oscillations start at 8° bTDC. Classify the event and give the interval from the spark.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "8° bTDC is later than 20° bTDC by 12°, so it is after the spark: knock, 12° after the spark."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "At 2400 rev/min the flame needs 1.0 ms to reach the end gas, whose ignition delay is 1.2 ms. Does the engine knock? Give both times in degrees.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "0.006 × 2400 = 14.4 °/ms. Flame: 14.4°; delay: 17.3°. The flame wins, so no knock."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "An engine knocks and then keeps running after the ignition is switched off. Explain the sequence and the cure.",
   "marks": 5,
   "a": "Persistent knock overheated a surface or built deposits; the hot spot now ignites the charge with no spark (run-on). Cure the cause of overheating and deposits, use the correct plug heat range and fuel; retarding the spark alone will not stop the hot spot."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "Why does knock lower power even though it releases energy quickly?",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "The pressure waves strip the cool boundary layer, so more heat goes into the walls; energy released in the wrong place and as oscillation does no useful work, and the controller must retard the spark."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Production engines use a piezoelectric knock sensor on the block and retard the spark of just the knocking cylinder for a few cycles. Highly boosted small engines also meet low-speed pre-ignition (LSPI), started by oil droplets, which has led to new oil standards. Search for knock sensor signal processing, LSPI and end-gas autoignition imaging.",
